{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Data, Information and Knowledge"}, {"type": "position", "content": 12}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear or use to talk about Data, Information and Knowledge."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Make the result report from the analysis of the information system, based on the customer requirements, following established rules and protocols."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": " Read and write about the communicative topics for this level through the appropriate use of simple pronunciation aspects, and grammar and vocabulary structures. Writes simple and short texts about models that respond to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Countable and Uncountable Nouns"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Countable nouns are the things you can put in an exact amount.  Uncountable nouns need a more specific unit of measure. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some nouns are countable; we can use them in singular and plural forms, and determine them with cardinal numbers. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">A</span> computer \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">The</span> computer(s) \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Many</span> computers \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Some</span> computers</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">An</span> image \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">The</span> image(s) \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Many</span> images \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Some</span> images </i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some other nouns are uncountable; we cannot determine them by using numbers, and they have no plural form."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">A piece of</span> advice \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">The</span> piece(s) of advice \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Much</span> advice \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Some</span> advice</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">An ounce of </span> prevention \u2013<span class=\"strong\">The</span> ounce(s) of prevention \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Much </span> prevention \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Some</span> prevention </i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "As you could see in the examples, we used articles and quantifiers at the beginning of each clause. Some of them work for both countable and uncountable nouns while some others not. Let's have a brief look at those words:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Definite article <span class=\"strong\">the</span>: We use <span class=\"strong\">the</span> definite article the to determine singular and plural."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">The</span> report \u2013 The reports</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Indefinite article <span class=\"strong\">a/an: </span> We use the indefinite article <span class=\"strong\">a/an </span> to determine a single unit of something; this means, we can only use it with countable nouns. Remember we choose between <span class=\"strong\">a </span> or <span class=\"strong\">an </span> depending on the first sound of the noun we determine."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">A </span> report \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">A </span> university \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">An </span>opinion \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">An </span> honest person</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Quantifier <span class=\"strong\">many: </span> We use this quantifier to express the existence of more than one thing when that thing is a countable noun."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">Many </span> reports \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Many </span> opinions</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Quantifier <span class=\"strong\">much: </span> We use this quantifier to express the existence of a set or bunch of something that is uncountable."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">Much </span> information \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Much </span> water</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Quantifier <span class=\"strong\">some: </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We can use this quantifier to determine both countable and uncountable nouns."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">Some </span> computers \u2013 <span class=\"strong\">Some </span> advice</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In today's lesson, we are going to talk about some differences between data, information, and knowledge."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Laura is accessing one of many web pages."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We know the stress load that carries performing many tasks at the same time and expose ourselves to much noise in our workplace."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Be Going to and Present Continuous With Time Expressions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\">be going to</span> to talk about future actions. We can use the <span class=\"strong\">present continuous</span> either to talk about future or present actions or plans. We can determine the time by using certain <span class=\"strong\">time expressions.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\">be going to</span> to express a future idea or plan when we intend to do something or have decided to do something but did not arrange it"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <span class=\"strong\">verb to be</span> + <span class=\"strong\">going to</span> + complement</div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use tThe <span class=\"strong\">present continuous </span> when we say what we have planned and arranged to do at a specific time in the future. When we use this structure, we focus on the arrangement of the activity."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <span class=\"strong\">verb to be </span> + <span class=\"strong\">verb \u2013ing </span> + complement</div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We can use certain <span class=\"strong\">time expressions </span> with both of these structures. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Time expressions </span> with the connotation of <span class=\"strong\">present </span> tense."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i>Now, right now, at present, currently, at the moment, nowadays, this year.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Time expressions </span> with the connotation of <span class=\"strong\">future </span> tense."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i>Tonight, tomorrow, today, on (Monday \u2013 Sunday), in (January \u2013 December) tonight, next week, next year.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">Now, </span> <span class=\"strong\">I'm going to </span> explain to you the difference between data and information.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <i>You <span class=\"strong\">are presenting </span> this topic <span class=\"strong\">on Wednesday. </span></i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<i><span class=\"strong\">At the moment, I <span class=\"strong\">am reading </span> an article on the difference between data, information and knowledge.</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "At this very moment, the information is real, but one month from now, it is going to be false. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "In today's lesson, we are going to talk about some differences between data, information, and knowledge."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Let's suppose now that Laura filled the form with the requested data, but she is moving to another country next month."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "reflection"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an image or representation of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Every fact that we perceive through our senses is data; data are a reflection and depiction of the world itself. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "further"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to a greater distance, or at a more advanced level"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  We are going to discuss further information next week."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "hypothetical situation"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a supposed fact"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Let's take a hypothetical situation as an example to explain this matter better."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "request"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to ask for something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There is a basic form that requests Laura's name, last name, age, address and nationality."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "capture"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to take something into your possession"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Information is how we capture data, and even though data is real, information can be false sometimes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob12aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Read about data, information and technology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe how they work."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a brochure showing the differences among each one."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Show your brochure to your classmates and teacher."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read about different concepts"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe differences among them"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write a brochure"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I show my brochure to my classmates and teacher"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le02ob12re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob12re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "In today's lesson, we are going to talk about some differences between data, information, and knowledge; they are some of the most important concepts related to technology and the internet. </br>\nData are facts. Every fact that we perceive through our senses is data; data are a reflection and depiction of the world itself. Information is how we capture data, and even though data is real, information can be false sometimes. Let's take a hypothetical situation as an example to explain this matter better."}, {"time": "38000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob12re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Laura is accessing one of many web pages. There is a basic form that requests Laura's name, last name, age, address and nationality. Every single fact about Laura is data, but the whole set that the web page stores is information. </br>\nLet's suppose that someone accesses the server and accidentally erases what Laura typed; this is a loss of information, not of data. Data are still the same; Laura is still Laura Williams, she's 26, she lives at 13 Oak Street, and she is American. However, the information is no longer there; the first difference between data and information is that the information can get lost but not the data. </br>Let's suppose now that Laura filled the form with the requested data, but she is moving to another country next month. At this very moment, the information is real, but one month from now, it is going to be false. Second difference: the information, as it reflects a single point in time, remains the same but the data change.</br>"}, {"time": "113000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob12re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Knowledge is a whole set of processes that a brain performs by using data and information. We know the stress load that carries performing many tasks at the same time and expose ourselves to much noise in our workplace. We can know it, maybe because we performed many tasks before and because someone told us their situation related to the exposition to much noise. We complement that information with every aspect related to it that we experience in our daily lives.</br>Please read the information I'm sending to your e-mails today in the afternoon; we are going to discuss further information next week."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le02ob12re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe8n3le02ob12re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 4, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can read about data, information and knowledge."}, {"type"